  "narrative": "Synthesis conclusion: the supplied dataset provides strong evidence for three central, dataset-specific predictor associations with composite dependency, and one additional secondary PTEN signal that is promising but less threshold-secure.\n\n1) SMARCA4 loss remains the clearest positive dependency association. H5 is accepted and independently validated: the signed estimate is +0.811 (independent interval +0.717 to +0.904), far above the 0.15-unit threshold. Because the claim direction is -1, the corresponding raw interpretation is that SMARCA4-loss samples have lower dependency_composite values, i.e. stronger composite dependency. This raw contrast also refutes the opposite-direction claim H1, which remains rejected.\n\n2) TP53 loss is robustly associated with weaker composite dependency. The overall TP53-loss claim H13 is accepted and independently validated at +0.803 (+0.712 to +0.895), and the TP53-loss-free-context TP53 claim H52 is now response-deadline reassessed and accepted at +0.811 (+0.723 to +0.899). Because these claims have direction +1, the raw interpretation is that TP53-loss samples have higher dependency_composite values, i.e. weaker dependency. Pairwise co-loss context claims H102-H103 remain large, so the TP53 association is not explained as a simple artifact of SMARCA4 or KEAP1 co-loss within this dataset.\n\n3) KEAP1 loss is robustly associated with weaker composite dependency. H14 remains accepted and independently validated at +0.747 (+0.654 to +0.840), and is now explicitly reassessed at its validation deadline. The raw interpretation is again higher dependency_composite values in KEAP1-loss samples, i.e. weaker dependency. Context claims H100-H101 and H103 support conditional stability in co-loss settings.\n\n4) PTEN loss is accepted as a dataset-specific secondary association in the TP53/SMARCA4/KEAP1-intact population, but it is the least secure accepted claim. H106 has discovery estimate +0.252 with discovery interval +0.169 to +0.335 and independent validation estimate +0.2525 with independent interval +0.1269 to +0.3783. The raw interpretation, given direction -1, is that PTEN-loss samples have lower dependency_composite values, i.e. stronger composite dependency. The two point estimates are nearly identical and both clearly exceed 0.15, and the discovery interval excludes the threshold. The independent validation interval, however, falls slightly below the threshold at its lower bound. Therefore I keep H106 accepted because the overall evidence supports a meaningful association in this dataset, but I keep it active and interpret it as requiring robustness confirmation: the association is supported, whereas precise threshold-exceedance is less fully pinned down by independent validation alone.\n\nNegative findings are substantial and informative. Broad main effects for KRAS, STK11, ARID1A, MTAP, MYC amplification, PIK3CA, EGFR amplification, RB1, and Avana library are rejected or not supported in their claimed directions. Within the triple-intact population, APC mutation, ERBB2 amplification, BRAF V600E, NF1 loss, SMAD4 loss, MTAP loss, ARID1A loss, STK11 loss, and RB1 loss are rejected. Pre-registered interaction tests do not support aneuploidy or high-Cas9 amplification of the core effects in the claimed directions. The earlier marginal reverse high-Cas9 KEAP1 attenuation signal H104 remains unresolved and is now closed because the stricter >=90% Cas9 test H118 failed to confirm it.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty remains for CDKN2A loss in the triple-intact population H105: signed estimate +0.144 with interval +0.083 to +0.204, just below the threshold in point estimate and straddling it in uncertainty. It remains active. Technical/contextual effects such as media, batch, library context, research markers, and broader generalizability remain unresolved or deferred. All conclusions are associations measured in this supplied dataset on a composite dependency endpoint, not causal knockout effects and not clinical generalizations.\n\nMost informative next analysis: confirm PTEN robustness in larger or biologically structured populations. I registered PTEN tests in each single core-predictor-intact population (TP53-loss-free, SMARCA4-loss-free, and KEAP1-loss-free), a PTEN test within triple-intact adenocarcinoma, and a focused CDKN2A follow-up within triple-intact adenocarcinoma. These follow-up analyses test whether PTEN remains meaningful beyond the triple-intact discovery setting and whether the borderline CDKN2A signal becomes more stable in the major subtype.",
  "research_notes": "Current final-state interpretation: accepted/independently validated core claims are SMARCA4 loss -> stronger composite dependency (H5), TP53 loss -> weaker composite dependency (H13; also stable in SMARCA4-intact context H52), and KEAP1 loss -> weaker composite dependency (H14). Co-loss context main effects H47-H52, H81-H83, H98-H103 show these effects remain large within relevant conditional populations. PTEN loss in triple-intact samples is accepted but active: discovery +0.252 with lower bound above threshold, validation +0.252 with lower bound just below threshold, so threshold exceedance is less secure than for core effects. CDKN2A triple-intact remains unresolved active. All other tested secondary triple-intact predictors are rejected. High-Cas9 interaction threads are closed after H118 failed; H104 remains unresolved closed. Negative core findings include KRAS main/interactions, opposite-direction TP53/KEAP1, STK11/ARID1A/MTAP broad main effects, MYC, PIK3CA, EGFR, RB1, Avana main effect, aneuploidy interactions, and high-Cas9 amplifying interactions. Next planned analyses prioritize PTEN robustness: TP53-intact, SMARCA4-intact, KEAP1-intact, triple-intact adenocarcinoma, plus triple-intact adenocarcinoma CDKN2A follow-up. Generalizable biological mechanism remains unknown; all statements are dataset-specific associations on a composite dependency endpoint."
